<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" dir="ltr" class="h-full bg-gray-100">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <%== assets(:css) %>
    <%== assets(:img) %>
    <%# Fathom rather than Google Analytics: no cookie, no personal data, no consent
        banner. Development and staging are kept out of the numbers by the allowed-domains
        firewall on the Fathom site rather than by a condition here, which is Fathom's own
        advice and is better than one -- it filters every hostname that is not the live
        one, including a local run that happens to boot with RACK_ENV=production.

        Deliberately not in views/oauth_layout.erb. That is the one page where a tap hands
        an API client the account, and the fewer origins in that moment the better. Since
        #142 that page's policy is default-src 'none' with no script-src at all, so this
        would be refused there outright; separate layouts mean leaving it out is the whole
        of it, and analytics_spec keeps it that way.

        It is the only third-party script the site loads, which is what this comment
        claimed while a tinyanalytics pixel sat twenty-two lines below it. Two vendors
        counting the same visits with different ideas of a session, different bot filtering
        and different sampling do not agree, so there was no answer to "how many people
        came this week" -- there were two, with no reason to prefer either. Fathom is the
        one #185 chose and said why: cookieless, no profile, no consent banner. The pixel
        was never part of that decision. analytics_spec now holds both halves, because the
        wrong half of this to get wrong is the comment: it is what somebody reads when
        deciding whether the CSP can be tightened. %>
    <script src="https://cdn.usefathom.com/script.js" data-site="VXQDPBHH" defer></script>

    <!-- HTML Meta Tags -->
    <title>tectonic plates, a lifting app</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Lift heavier with tectonic plates. Track your workouts and progress.">
    <%# One domain. render.yaml serves this app at tectonicplates.app and still allows
        tectonic.onrender.com, so without this the same page is two pages. %>
    <link rel="canonical" href="<%= canonical_url %>">
    <%# The tab, the bookmark, and the iOS home screen -- which matters more here than on
        most sites, because a gym app that gets added to a home screen is a gym app that
        gets opened, and it was a blank document glyph until #254.

        Three files because browsers still disagree. icon.svg is the real one and scales to
        anything; favicon.ico carries 16, 32 and 48 for what cannot read it; and
        apple-touch-icon.png is opaque at 180x180 because iOS composites a transparent icon
        onto black. All three are the plates from _logo.erb with the wordmark dropped,
        since eleven characters cannot be read at sixteen pixels.

        theme-color paints the phone browser's own chrome, so the bar above the page is the
        app's blue instead of white. %>
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32">
    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#075985">

    <%# Both cards fetch their image from this origin. They asked tectonic.onrender.com for
        it until #251, which is the sort of thing that keeps working until the day the old
        subdomain is retired and then breaks everywhere at once, silently, in somebody
        else's cache. The alt text is here because a card announced nothing at all to a
        screen reader, and og:site_name because it was attributed to nobody. %>
    <!-- Facebook Meta Tags -->
    <meta property="og:url" content="<%= canonical_url %>">
    <meta property="og:type" content="website">
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="tectonic plates">
    <meta property="og:title" content="tectonic plates, a lifting app">
    <meta property="og:description" content="Lift heavier with tectonic plates. Track your workouts and progress.">
    <meta property="og:image" content="<%= Tectonic::CANONICAL_ORIGIN %>/img/screenshot.jpeg">
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="A tectonic plates workout, with each set's weight and the plates that make it.">

    <!-- Twitter Meta Tags -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta property="twitter:domain" content="tectonicplates.app">
    <meta property="twitter:url" content="<%= canonical_url %>">
    <%# There is no separate product account, so the site and its author are one handle. %>
    <meta name="twitter:site" content="@stephanieblack">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="tectonic plates, a lifting app">
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="Lift heavier with tectonic plates. Track your workouts and progress.">
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="<%= Tectonic::CANONICAL_ORIGIN %>/img/screenshot.jpeg">
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="A tectonic plates workout, with each set's weight and the plates that make it.">

    <%# Charting costs a quarter of a megabyte, and on a page that draws no chart it is a
        quarter of a megabyte of nothing -- the login form, the sign-up form and every
        workout page carried it. A view that charts says so by setting @charts, which is
        set by the time this runs because Roda renders the template first and this layout
        around it. Chart.js is named before Chartkick, which adapts it. The vendored
        chartjs-adapter-date-fns bundle, 215KB of it, is not loaded at all: it teaches
        Chart.js to read dates for a time axis, and the one chart in the app plots its
        days as evenly spaced categories, so nothing ever parses a date. A chart that
        wants a real calendar will need it back. %>
    <%- if @charts %>
      <script src="/js/chart.umd.js"></script>
      <script src="/js/chartkick.js"></script>
    <%- end %>
    <%# And the same for htmx, which is 50KB and was loaded on every page in the app while
        being used on three. /exercises, /volume, /programs and /about each parsed and ran
        it to do nothing at all -- and the front page and /about are the two most likely
        first visits, neither of which uses it.

        A partial that carries hx- attributes sets @htmx, so the answer is written where
        the attributes are rather than in a route that has to remember. The risk of getting
        this wrong is quiet -- buttons that stop swapping and fall back to full page posts,
        which still work -- so spec/htmx_loading_spec.rb walks the app and holds the two
        together instead of leaving it to be noticed. %>
    <%- if @htmx %>
      <script src="/js/htmx.min.js"></script>
    <%- end %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%# First in the body on purpose: the first Tab press must land here, before the
        eight nav links a keyboard or screen-reader user otherwise steps through on
        every page. sr-only until it holds focus, so nobody else ever sees it. %>
    <a href="#main"
       class="<%= button_style %> sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:left-4 focus:top-4
              focus:z-50 bg-sky-800 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white">Skip to content</a>
  <%== render('nav') %>
    <main id="main">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4">
        <%== yield %>
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
